  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/9e236591e21403bb739dafa68a665c8a10eed0e9e8ac104e7b39431f02329c17.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
